<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Tenkasi Pandyas</h1><p class="st-meta">1422 CE – 1650 CE (approx.) · capital Tenkasi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A late branch of the Pandya dynasty that survived in the far south of Tamil Nadu, ruling from Tenkasi under Vijayanagara and Nayak overlords.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Later Pandyan Empire</a> lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/madurai-sultanate">Madurai Sultanate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> princes continued to hold small territories in the Tirunelveli region. In the 15th century one branch settled at Tenkasi, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arikesari-parakrama-pandya">Arikesari Parakrama Pandya</a> began the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasi-viswanathar-temple-tenkasi">Kasi Viswanathar</a> temple in the 1440s.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Tenkasi <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Pandyas</a> ruled as subordinate chiefs, first of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> and then of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/madurai-nayak-dynasty">Madurai Nayak dynasty</a>, rather than as independent kings. Their importance lay in culture: several were poets in Tamil, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ativira-rama-pandya">Ativira Rama Pandya</a>, author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naidatham">Naidatham</a>, and they endowed temples in Tenkasi and Tirunelveli.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The line faded into obscurity during the 17th century as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/madurai-nayak-dynasty">Madurai Nayaks</a> consolidated their rule over the south, and its last members are poorly documented. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasi-viswanathar-temple-tenkasi">Kasi Viswanathar</a> temple at Tenkasi remains their chief monument.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tenkasi-pandyas.jpg" alt="Tenkasi Pandyas" width="320" height="180"><figcaption>Image: Jayarathina · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Twin_fish_flag_of_Pandyas.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1422 CE – 1650 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tenkasi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Arikesari Parakrama Pandya, Ativira Rama Pandya</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism (Shaivism)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tamil</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Later Pandyas of Tenkasi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1536&amp;focus=tenkasi-pandyas" aria-label="Tenkasi Pandyas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6815 1843 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tenkasi Pandyas lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7135 2099L7154 2063M7135 2099L7088 1864M7135 2099L7154 2063"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7154 2063h0M7088 1864h0M7154 2063h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7135 2099h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7135 2099h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tenkasi. <a href="/atlas?year=1536&amp;focus=tenkasi-pandyas">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
